<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seti I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1290–1279 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a> · Also known as Menmaatre, Sethi, Sety Merenptah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Seti I was the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the Nineteenth Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, ruling from 1290 to 1279 BCE, the son of Ramesses I and father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a>. After the upheavals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>'s reign he worked to restore order and Egyptian power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> several times and took the Syrian town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Kadesh</a> and the land of Amurru, which soon returned to Hittite control. He built a mortuary temple at Qurna on the west bank at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> and began a white limestone temple at Abydos that his son completed. His tomb in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-the-kings">Valley of the Kings</a>, KV17, found by Giovanni Belzoni in 1817, is the longest of the New Kingdom royal tombs.</p></div>
